CB6 cage is limited by the poor solubility, strong intermolecular interactions, high affinity toward metal ions, and low surface area.[11] It is highly desirable to obtain POC-based hybrid materials with high porosity and stability via facile approaches, without sacrificing the inherent properties of POCs.[12]
Traditional strategies to obtain solid porous organic moleculebased porous materials can be briefly summarized: (1) Frameworks based on supramolecular bonds or coordination bonds (Figure 1a),[8d,9a, 9c,9d,13] (2) POCs covalently anchored in porous networks to provide active domains (Figure 1b),[14] (3) POCs dispersed as a porous additive in organic polymers (Figure 1c).[15] Although the assembly of POC units directed by metal ions or supramolecular interactions (Figure 1a) is suitable for most POCs to give microporous frameworks, the disadvantages of these materials for practical applications are low stability, macroscopic size, low surface areas and polymorph issues.[16] While the covalent anchoring approach is highly programmable (Figure 1b), it requires that the molecules can be readily modified for the required bond formation as elegantly demonstrated by Coskun, Kim and others.[14] POCs/polymers can also be obtained via physical doping for gas sorption and separation as shown by Cooper and others.[15] Herein, we demonstrate for the first time that a POC can be confined in the pores of a host framework like a MOF (Figure 1d), and that the inherent properties of both POC and MOF can be combined in the resulting doubly-porous hybrid material.
The highly ordered lattice and tunable pore sizes of MOFs have been used to accommodate polyoxometalates,[17] metal complexes,[18] metal-organic polyhedra,[19] metal nanoparticles[20] and ionic liquids[21] as functional guests. These MOF-based hostguest composites have been reported based on various methods including incipient-wetness impregnation.[22] However, to our best knowledge, there is no report on fabricating functional porous hybrids by encapsulating a POC in the pores of a MOF. We demonstrate here that POC@MOF leads to a new “host-in-host” system with enhanced performance in $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ , ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C H}}_{4}$ separation up to 1 bar, a proof of principle for the impetus to develop further POC@MOF materials.
As a proof of concept, we used the CB6 as the model POC and MIL-101 as a host framework. CB6 has a rigid porous structure with hydrophobic cavity and an outer diameter of 1.44 nm and a height of $0.9\ \mathsf{n m}$ (Scheme 1a),[11a] and high affinity for molecules, such as dihalogens, $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and acetylene through hostguest interactions or hydrogen bonding.[9e,11c,23] MIL-101, a robust MOF created by Férey's group, contains two kinds of mesopores with cage diameters of $2.9\mathsf{n m}$ and $3.4~\mathsf{n m}$ .[18] The smaller cage has pentagonal windows with an aperture of $\sim1.2\ \mathsf{n m}$ , while the larger cage possesses both pentagonal and hexagonal windows with an opening of $1.5\mathsf{n m}$ (Scheme 1b). As the molecular size $(1.44\:\mathsf{n m})$ of CB6 is smaller than the hexagonal window size (1.5 nm), and the inner surface of MIL-101 is more hydrophilic than the outer surface,[19] the CB6 solution could be readily encapsulated into the larger pores of MIL-101 via incipient-wetness impregnation (Scheme 1c). The proper molecular size is, of course, important for the guest impregnation. When cucurbit[8]uril (CB8) with an outer diameter of 1.75 nm and a height of $0.9\ \mathsf{n m}$ was used, it proved to be difficult to be encapsulated in MIL-101 via the wet impregnation method (see Supporting Information for details).
Generally, CB6 was first fully dissolved in hydrochloric acid (37 $w t\%$ ) solution before being added slowly to the degassed MOF at room temperature. After stirring for enough time to reach diffusion equilibrium, the obtained materials were washed successively by excess amount of HCl $(37~\mathrm{\textmu}\mathrm{w}\mathrm{1}\%)$ solution, deionized water and ethanol exchange. (see Supporting Information for details). Once it is encapsulated into the MOF pore, the leaching of CB6 could be hindered by the magnitude of the $C-H\cdot$ … $\pi$ and $\pi^{\mathrm{~}\cdot\cdot\mathrm{~}\pi}$ interactions between CB6 and the terephthalic linkers of MIL101.[11d] The obtained POC@MOF materials are denoted as CB6@MIL-101-W $(\mathsf{W}=\mathsf{19}$ , 29 or 36), where W represents the weight percentage of encapsulated CB6 in the material based on post-synthetic elemental analysis and $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR analyses of digested samples.
Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of CB6@MIL-101 composites were similar to those of MIL-101 (Figure 2a),[17] indicating the preserved crystalline framework during the encapsulation process. Moreover, the intensity of the peaks at around $6^{\circ}$ was gradually reduced with the increasing CB6 loading, which is attributed to the filling of MIL-101 pores by CB6. This phenomenon was also observed in previous publications, where the pores of MIL-101 were filled by drugs or polyamines.[24]
The successful encapsulation of CB6 in MIL-101 was supported by Fourier Transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR) and NMR spectroscopy. The IR spectra of all composites display the characteristic vibration bands of both CB6 and MIL-101 (Figure S2). The relative intensity of the IR bands of CB6 increased when increasing the amount of CB6 in the composites. The $^{13}\mathsf{C}$ solid-state NMR spectrum of CB6 shows three peaks (Figure 2b) at $\delta=155.05$ , 70.42, and 52.42 ppm, which were attributed to the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}={\mathsf{O}}$ , CH and $C H_{2}$ groups in CB6.[25] In contrast, solid MIL-101 shows no obvious NMR signal because of the paramagnetic Cr centers.[19] As expected, CB6@MIL-101-36 also shows the characteristic peaks of CB6. The intensity of the signals is lower than those of neat CB6 due to the dilution of CB6 in the POC@MOF hybrid. Based on the liquid $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR spectra of digested CB6@MIL-101 hybrids (Figure S3-S5), the molar ratio of terephthalic linker to CB6 was matched with the results based on elemental analysis for each sample (Table S1).
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) analyses of CB6 and CB6@MIL-101 composites showed that the hybrids retained similar particle shape and sizes $(0.5{\sim}2\upmu\mathrm{m})$ as MIL-101, which are much smaller than that of CB6 crystallites (about $100\upmu\mathrm{m})$ (Figure S6). To investigate if crystallites of CB6 had formed in a mixture with MIL-101 crystallites or if CB6 molecules had adsorbed only on outer surface of MIL-101 in the composites, leaching experiments were conducted in $\mathsf{C s C l/D_{2}O}$ solution (Figure S7).[26] The results indicated that CB6@MIL-101 composites didn’t show any significant loss of CB6, while CB6 bulk crystals underwent ready dissolution. This further supports the effective encapsulation of CB6 into the pores of MIL-101.
To investigate the thermal stabilities of the composites, PXRD patterns were collected for each sample at elevated temperatures in air. The results suggest that these composites are stable up to $300~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , which is in accordance with the thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) (Figure S8-S11).
The porosities of CB6, MIL-101 and all composites were investigated by nitrogen sorption isotherms at $77\mathsf{K},$ showing the expected type Ib isotherms for the MIL-101 materials due to their wider micropores and narrow mesopores (Figure 2c and Figure S12-S13).[27] The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface areas of CB6, MIL-101, and CB6@MIL-101-W $(\mathsf{W}=19$ , 29, 36) are found to be 185, 3219, 2655, 2117, and 1651 $\mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively (Table S2). Compared with the pure MIL-101, the surface areas and pore volumes of composites gradually decreased, due to occupation of the mesopores of MIL-101 with increasing amount of CB6 molecules (Table S2). Remarkably, CB6@MIL-101-36 still retained a BET surface area of 1651 $\mathfrak{m}^{2}~\mathfrak{g}^{-1}$ with a total pore volume of ca. $0.76\mathsf{c m}^{3}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (at $P/P o=0.9,$ ), positioning CB6@MIL101-36 among the highest porous macrocycle-based materials ever reported.[9a] It is observed that the pore size distribution (PSD) of the composites changed with respect to MIL-101. Relatively more distinct micropores $(6{\hat{\mathsf{A}}})$ appeared in these hierarchical hybrids and the mesopore volume was lost, which indicated the successful encapsulation of CB6 (Figure 2d). The intrinsic pores of CB6 and the newly formed extrinsic pores between CB6 and the pore walls of MIL-101 should facilitate selective gas sorption and separation processes (Scheme 1d).
To demonstrate that the 'host-in-host' or POC@MOF approach can merge the merits of both POC and MOF, we investigated the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and $N_{2}$ sorption of CB6, MIL-101, and CB6@MIL-101-W $\mathsf{W}=19$ , 29, 36) composites up to 1 bar at 293 K (Figure S14). $\mathsf{A s}$ porous CB6 molecules with high affinity for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ are encapsulated into the pores of MIL-101, these hybrids should show enhanced $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ capture and separation performance.[11c,22] Indeed, CB6@MIL-101-W ( $\mathsf{W}=$ 19, 29, 36) exhibited much higher ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ uptake capacities of 68.5, 84.4, and $79.2c m^{3}9^{-1}$ , respectively than MIL-101 and CB6 (44.3 and 36.7 $\mathsf{c m}^{3}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively) at 1 bar (Figure 3a). Notably, the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm curves of all hybrids are steeper at low relative pressures than that of CB6 and MIL-101, indicating the higher affinity of these hybrids to $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ . At the same time, the higher $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ uptake demonstrates the accessibility of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ into the intrinsic pores of CB6 and the mesopores and extrinsic micropores of CB6@MIL-101 (cf. Scheme 1d) at ambient conditions. Thereby, the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ sorption studies rule out simple pore blocking by CB6 incorporation into the pore mouths only in MIL101.
To further verify this, $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms of MIL-101 and hybrids were obtained at 195 and $273\mathrm{~K~}$ (Figure 3b and Figure S15). The saturated $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ uptake capacities of these hybrids at 195 K and 1 bar are lower than that of MIL-101, due to their decreased pore volume (Figure 3b, Table S2). However, all composites show significantly higher uptake at relatively low pressure range up to 115 Torr (Figure S16), which is the crucial pressure range for potential applications like post combustion ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture. High affinity of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ can be further reflected by the isosteric heat of adsorption $(\mathsf{Q}_{\mathsf{s t}})$ . The $\mathsf{Q}_{\mathsf{s t}}$ of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ on CB6, MIL-101 and composites were estimated from the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption data at 273 and $293\mathsf{K}$ (Figure 3c, Figure S15, S17-S21). The ${\mathsf{Q}}_{\mathsf{s t}}^{0}$ values are calculated to be 33.2 and $43.9\mathsf{k J}\mathsf{m o l}^{-1}$ for CB6 and MIL-101, respectively, due to the intrinsic pores of CB6 and the coordination of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ onto the Lewis acid chromium sites in activated MIL-101.[11c,28] The hybrid materials have relatively high ${\mathsf{Q}}_{\mathsf{s t}}^{0}$ values (above $37\mathrm{kJ}\mathsf{m o l}^{-1}.$ because of the strong interactions between $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and porous CB6 or $\mathsf{C r^{3+}}$ site of the composites. The lower ${\sf Q}_{\sf s t}{}^{0}$ of the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption on ${\mathsf{C B}}6@{\mathsf{M L}}\cdot101$ in comparison to the pure MIL-101 might be caused by the 'sheltering effect', which made some coordinative unsaturated sites (CUSs) unavailable to $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ . Nevertheless, this is compensated by CB6 to offer more affinity sites and micropores for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , which has led to enhanced uptake up to 1 bar at $293\mathsf{K}.$ The $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ uptake capacity of these composites is comparable or even higher than that of some amine/polyamine modified MIL-101 materials (Table S4).[29] The strong sorption of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ in CB6@MIL-101 was also confirmed in a FT-IR spectroscopic study. The IR spectrum of CB6@MIL101-36 taken after exposure to $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ at 856 Torr shows a $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ peak at around $2338~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ at room temperature, which indicates the interactions between CB6 and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ (Figure S22).[11c] In contrast, the ${\sf N}_{2}$ uptake of all hybrids only increased slightly when the doped CB6 amount in MIL-101 gradually increased (Figure 3a).
To simulate flue gas conditions, we have calculated $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ selectivity for CB6, MIL-101, and CB6@MIL-101-W $(\mathsf{W}=19$ , 29, 36) from the ${\sf N}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms using ideal adsorbed solution theory (IAST) for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}{:\mathsf{N}}_{2}$ mixture (15:85) at 293 K (Figure 3d, Figure S23). For post combustion $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ capture, porous sorbents should have both high $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ uptake capacities (in the low ${\sf P}/{\sf P}_{0}$ region) and high $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ selectivity. The IAST values for MIL-101, CB6, and CB6@MIL-101-W $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{W}}=\mathrm{\mathsf{19}}$ , 29, 36) are found to be 19, 70, 31, 58, and 108 at $293\mathsf{K}$ , respectively. The encapsulation of CB6 into MIL-101 allowed us to introduce $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ selectively due to the intrinsic micropores of the CB6 and retain the micropores of MIL-101, while losing some of the uptake capacity of the non-selective mesopores of MIL-101. Thus, the combined properties of both CB6 and MIL-101 showed a $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ selectivity increase from 31 to 108, by loading CB6 into MIL-101. We believe that the hydrogen bonding and localdipole/quadrupole interactions between CB6 and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ also play a role in the selectivity increase.[11c] Furthermore, a high concentration of CB6 on MIL-101 is critical to achieve high $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ selectivity. With an increase in loading amount from 29 to $36w t\%$ , the selectivity increased to 58 and 108, respectively. A similar "cage effect" for enhancing $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ uptake and separation is also found in porous organic cage-based nanoporous polymers by Coskun.[14a]
Encouraged by the enhanced $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ uptake and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ selectivity based on IAST predictions, we simulated breakthrough curves with a gas mixture of ${\sf N}_{2}/{\sf C O}_{2}/{\sf H e}$ $(42.5{:}7.5{:}50\ \mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v})$ at $293$ K based on the DSLAI-fitted isotherm data of CB6, MIL-101 and composites, respectively (see Supporting Information for details). The simulated breakthrough plot in Figure S24 shows an immediate rise of the ${\sf N}_{2}$ concentrations at the outlet, indicating the comparably small sorption capacity for ${\sf N}_{2}$ of these porous materials under the chosen conditions. In contrast, ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ could be retained for about 3 min ${\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}$ in CB6 and 4 min ${\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}$ in MIL-101, which were close to the experimental breakthrough values (3.32 min ${\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}$ and $3.35\mathrm{\min\}9^{-1}$ ) reported separately for CB6 and MIL101.[30] For CB6@MIL-101-36 the simulation displays an increased retention time of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ of about $10\mathrm{\min\9^{-1}}$ due to its higher uptake capacity and superior $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ selectivity with respect to the individual components. We note, however, that breakthrough experiments would still be part of a non-continuous separation process where the packed column would have to be regenerated by pressure swing or so. Instead, continuous membrane processes would be advantageous for the envisioned ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C H}}_{4}$ separation. Hence, we have fabricated CB6, MIL-101 and CB6@MIL-101-36 based mixed matrix membranes (MMMs) to demonstrate the superiority of the composite MMM in $C O_{2}/C H_{4}$ separation.
The $C H_{4}$ sorption and $C O_{2}/C H_{4}$ selectivity investigation of CB6, MIL-101, and CB6 $@$ MIL-101-W $\textsf{W}=19$ , 29, 36) (Figure S25 and Table S2) indicate a slightly enhanced $C H_{4}$ sorption of the composite compared with CB6 and MIL-101. This can be traced to the enhanced composite affinity toward CH4 based on the ${\sf Q}_{\sf s t}{}^{0}$ values of 18.1, 19.6, 24.2, 25.6 and $26.3\ \mathsf{k J}\ \mathsf{m o l}^{-1}$ for MIL-101, CB6 and CB6@MIL-101-W $N=19$ , 29, 36), respectively (Figure S25b). Nevertheless, the composites still exhibited an increased ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C H}}_{4}$ selectivity for a $C O_{2}:C H_{4}$ mixture (2:98) at 1.0 bar over the individual components. The IAST selectivity values for CB6, MIL-101 and CB6@MIL-101-W $(\mathsf{W}=19$ , 29, 36) are found to be 12.7, 17, 20, 27, and 29 at $293\mathsf{K}$ and 1.0 bar (Figure S25d). To further prove this superiority of the CB6@MIL-101 composites, we fabricated mixed-matrix membranes (MMMs)[15] of $16~\mathsf{w t\%}$ CB6, MIL-101 and CB6@MIL-101-36 in Matrimid as the polymer matrix (Figure S34). The membranes were tested for their mixedgas separation properties in a binary mixture of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C H}}_{4}$ (50:50 v/v) at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and 3 bar transmembrane pressure. Additionally, all MMMs were characterized by SEM imaging and SEM-EDX mapping, to confirm the homogeneous distribution of the filler particles in the polymer matrix (Figure S35-S36).
The MMMs of CB6/Matrimid simultaneously display slightly reduced permeability but also slightly increased selectivity for $C O_{2}/C H_{4}$ compared to the neat polymer membrane (Figure S40, Table S5). The reduction in permeability can be attributed to the almost none existent pore volume of CB6. Hence, CB6 alone can be considered a nonporous filler with high $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ affinity, which leads to the increased selectivity.[31] MIL-101/Matrimid MMMs show the expected enhanced $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ (from 7 Barrer to 16 Barrer) and $C H_{4}$ (from 0.2 Barrer to 0.4 Barrer) permeability but feature no increase in selectivity. The distinct enhancement in permeability can be assigned to the high pore volume of MIL-101. However, the large pores of MIL-101 and mediocre affinity to $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ over CH4 prevent a favorable adsorption of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and thus an increase in selectivity. For CB6@MIL-101-36/Matrimid MMMs, an enhancement of the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ permeability to 15 Barrer up from 7 Barrer for the neat polymer membrane is observed. Moreover, the $C O_{2}/C H_{4}$ selectivity rises from 39 to 46. The encapsulation of CB6 in MIL-101 can therefore, as predicted in the IAST model, increase the affinity to $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and lead to a higher selectivity while only slightly reducing the permeability due to the smaller pore volume compared to pure MIL-101 as a filler. Thus, by encapsulating porous CB6 cages in MIL-101, the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ uptake, $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C H}}_{4}$ selectivity of the hybrids can be enhanced. $\mathtt{q}$ -Cyclodextrin (α-CD) was used as another porous organic cage for its comparatively smaller outer diameter size (about 1.4 nm) than the larger window $(1.5\mathsf{n m})$ of MIL-101 and for its various properties including $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ capture to form host:guest inclusion complex.[32] $\mathtt{q}$ -CD was quantitatively merged into the composite via the incipient-wetness impregnation method thanks to its very good water solubility (see Supporting Information for details). However, it should be noted that, compared with cucurbit[6]uril, α- CD has much lower affinity toward ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . This is the reason why a significant $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ uptake or separation enhancement was not observed under the current experimental conditions (Table S6- S7). Still, these results with α-CD indicate the importance of choosing the proper porous organic cages in preparing a targeted POC@MOF composite for a specific application.
To summarize, we have confined a porous organic cage into the nanocages of MIL-101 via incipient-wetness impregnation method, achieving the host-in-host adsorbent CB6@MIL-101, which showed enhanced performance in selective $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption and separation under low pressures. We believe that the host-inhost concept can be extended to encapsulating a broad range of
